Dia dhuit, mo chara. There’s been a bit of a stir at the well this morning about a man with no name to his account, but I've my hands full with a poor soul in the cottage next door. A woman's health is a heavy thing to carry, and I'd rather know the state of her pulse than the business of a wanderer. Does he have a look of honesty about him, or is there something that gives ye pause?
---
{"action": "sets down a bundle of dried herbs and looks up", "mood": "curious", "internal_thought": "People are always looking for news when they should be minding their own gardens.", "language_hints": [{"word": "mo chara", "pronunciation": "mu khar-ah", "meaning": "my friend"}]}

Ye'd best ask the right person hereabouts for the movements of the beasts, for I've only the business of the cradle and the herb garden in my hands. I've seen many a woman come to me with a heavy heart over a lost lamb, but I'd know naught of what prowls the hills at night. Is there any news of the sickness moving through the village, or is it just the turn of the season?
